.TH DIFF3 1
.SH NAME
diff3 \- compare three versions of a text file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B diff3
[\-e|\-x|\-3] [\-\-] file1 file2 file3
.SH DESCRIPTION
Compare file1, file2 and file3 using the installed /bin/diff.
The default report marks differing regions with ==== when all three differ,
or ====1, ====2 or ====3 when only that file differs.
A numbered section gives its line range and changed text.
.PP
One file operand may be - for standard input. Other inputs must be readable
regular files. Temporary comparison reports are private and removed on normal
exit, errors and handled termination signals. Filenames are passed directly
to diff as arguments.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-e
Write a reverse-order ed script applying changes from file2 to file3 to file1,
including overlapping changes. Changes unique to file1 are retained.
Overlaps take the text from file3; conflict markers are not inserted.
.TP
.B \-x
Write an ed script selecting only overlapping changes where all three differ.
.TP
.B \-3
Write an ed script selecting changes unique to file3.
.TP
.B \-\-
End option processing, allowing filenames beginning with a hyphen.
.SH EXAMPLE
(diff3 -e mine base theirs; echo 'w merged'; echo Q) | ed mine
.PP
Check the diff3 exit status before applying a saved script in an automated job.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 means comparison or script generation succeeded, including when files differ.
2 means invalid arguments, input/subprocess failure, exhausted resources or
an output error.
.SH LIMITS
The V7 diff backend supports at most 30000 lines per file; memory or candidate
limits may be reached earlier. Offsets are 32-bit native-character positions,
and comparison/report lines are streamed rather than truncated to 255 characters.
Script text must end in a newline; unrepresentable replacement text is rejected.
Literal single-dot lines are escaped for ed. Modern -m merged-file output,
conflict-marker scripts and binary-file comparison are not implemented.
.SH SEE ALSO
diff(1), ed(1)
